From 3d70f44b8a9dca6d1ed98e86db567010de4c54e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 15:40:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix exponential walks and a stack overflow in native code
 block checks

Three checks in the native code generator walk the flow graph
recursively and visited a block once per path instead of once. A run of
N independent conditionals has 2^N paths through it, so the compile
time doubled or tripled with every conditional added
(IsSameRegisterSource at every native optimisation level, the other two
above -O0); one of the three could also recurse without end.

IsSameRegisterSource (does the A, X or Y register already hold the
value this load gives, on every path into the block; for
PartialBackwardValuePropagation and BackwardValuePropagation) walks all
predecessors and stops at the first failure. It now keeps the (block,
loop count) pairs it has verified. A pair seen again returns true, as
it did the first time, because a failure would have ended the walk; the
loop count is part of the key because it changes the answer at a loop
head.

CanJoinEntryLoadStoreZP (can a zero page copy move up into the blocks
in front of a join) has the same shape and gets a visited set per
check. Its result depends only on the block, and it still sets mChecked
on every block it reaches, so DoJoinEntryLoadStoreZP changes the same
blocks as before.

CheckSingleUseGlobalLoadStruct (can a load be forwarded to the uses of
the local it was stored in) is guarded by mPatched, but since 1073206
(Fix too strong check for native structure copy forwarding) a block
first reached behind an overwrite of the local is walked a second time,
as poisoned, when a path reaches it without an overwrite, and nothing
recorded that this had been done: every such path walked the block and
everything behind it again. With a run of conditionals behind the block
that is the same exponential walk. With a loop behind it that does not
store to the local, the second walk came back to the block it started
from, started again, and overflowed the stack: the compiler crashed on
element_loop() of the new test.

The second walk is now done once per block (mPatchRecheck). An arrival
at a block whose second walk is still running fails as before: such a
block has mPatchUsed set, and a second walk that completed has left it
clear, since a poisoned walk fails at the first use it finds. The
mPatchFail term added to that test is defensive: every arrival inside a
second walk is poisoned or cleared, so the old condition already fails
there. Repeating the second walk had one more effect, which is kept:
started again from a block inside it, it could pass a block that
overwrites the local and arrive, cleared, back at the block whose
second walk was running, and fail there. This happens when an
overwriting block leads straight back into a block that is walked a
second time after it, so the overwriting block marks its successors
(mPatchRejoin) and a marked block searches the blocks behind it for the
overwriting block (CheckSingleUseGlobalLoadStructLoop). The check gives
the same answer as before wherever the old one came back; where it
overflowed the stack the answer is now false, which leaves the store in
place.

The test has one function per walk with 16, 24 and 14 conditionals,
the loop that overflowed the stack, and four more shapes of the
structure check with asserts on the values. The functions are called
with more than one argument value so that the arguments are not folded
into them. Without the fix the native configurations above -O0 crash
after several seconds and -O0 takes a few seconds; with it each of the
ten configurations compiles in about half a second or less.

